															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kothardarastrix" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3460794); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3460794&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609284691" alt="Kothardarastrix" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3460794)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kothardarastrix" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3460794); return false;" >Kothardarastrix</a></span> <span class="odate time_1608098929 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Dec 2020 06:08</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4845925">
									

<p>It does seem odd that 682 adapts to non existence but not acid, but remember that water bears can survive exposure to outer space but not being eaten by snails.</p>
<p>I think 682 evolved regeneration as a general catch-all way to not die and only manifests additional abilities when the regeneration is insufficient to prevent death. It doesn’t need acid resistance to avoid being killed by the acid, since its regeneration is enough to keep it alive already. To recover from getting chucked into the void, it has to come up with something more creative than that. 682 isn’t an unstoppable Doomsday-like juggernaut that adapts to everything to make itself more powerful; rather, it operates on similar principles to real animals and only adapts as necessary to survive.</p>
<p>As for why 682 doesn’t kill 173, that’s not just because it’s a sacred cow. 682’s reaction implies all sorts of horrible things about 173. Just think: Why, in universe, wouldn’t 682 destroy the statue? What does it know that we don’t? This the is the uncomfortable question the author wanted you to ask yourself when you read that.</p>

							</div>

								
